Container Base Image
================================================================================
The published container image is built FROM the digest-pinned alpine:3.24 base
and includes, at runtime, the following unmodified OS packages installed via apk
(not Go dependencies, so not covered by THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md):

  xfsprogs-extra  (main/xfsprogs)       — LGPL-2.1-or-later
  quota-tools     (community)           — GPL-2.0-or-later
  e2fsprogs       (main)                — GPL-2.0-or-later AND LGPL-2.0-or-later
                                           AND BSD-3-Clause AND MIT
  util-linux      (main)                — GPL-3.0-or-later AND GPL-2.0-or-later AND
                                           GPL-2.0-only AND GPL-1.0-only AND
                                           LGPL-2.1-or-later AND BSD-1-Clause AND
                                           BSD-3-Clause AND BSD-4-Clause-UC AND MIT
                                           AND Public-Domain
  btrfs-progs     (main)                — GPL-2.0-or-later

The alpine:3.24 base is pinned by digest in Dockerfile. The exact package
name-version actually shipped in a given build, as reported by `apk info -a`
at build time, is baked into the image itself at
/licenses/os-packages-manifest.txt. Read that file (or inspect the matching
package in Alpine's package index) to identify the precise Corresponding Source
for the image you have. Source for every version of each package is unmodified
and publicly available from Alpine's aports repository
(https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports).

Full text of every GNU license family used above (GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0, LGPL-2.0,
LGPL-2.1) is included in this image at /licenses/gnu/.

These GPL/LGPL-licensed tools are invoked by nfs-quota-agent as separate OS
processes (fork/exec of xfs_quota, setquota, chattr, findmnt, btrfs — see
quota.CommandRunner) and are aggregated, unmodified, alongside the Apache-2.0
licensed Go binary in the same image; they are not linked into it. Under the
FSF's own guidance on "mere aggregation" (GPL FAQ), this does not extend GPL
terms to nfs-quota-agent's own source, which remains Apache-2.0 licensed in
full.

Redistribution of the packages themselves is a separate obligation, and the
aports link above does not by itself discharge it: GPL-2.0 §3 requires the
distributor to either accompany the binaries with source, or extend a written
offer for it. GPL-3.0 §6(d) does permit pointing at a network server, but the
older packages here are GPL-2.0. Accordingly:

